- Family type: Joint families might provide more social support or sometimes more stress depending on relationships; nuclear families might be more isolated or supportive depending on context.
- Breastfeed: Breastfeeding is often linked with better mother-infant bonding and possibly lower risk of postpartum depression.
- Angry after latest child birth: Feeling angry after childbirth can be a sign of emotional distress, possibly linked to postpartum depression.
- Major changes or losses during pregnancy: Experiencing major losses or changes during pregnancy can increase stress and risk of postpartum depression.
- Occupation After Your Latest Childbirth: Occupation might influence stress levels and social interaction; housewives might have different stressors compared to working mothers.
- Fear of pregnancy: Fear related to pregnancy can increase anxiety and risk of postpartum depression.
- Trust and share feelings: Inability to trust and share feelings can indicate poor social support, increasing risk of postpartum depression.
- Husband’s monthly income: Lower income might increase financial stress, increasing risk of postpartum depression.
- Relationship with the in-laws: Poor or bad relationships can increase stress, increasing risk of postpartum depression.
- Pregnancy length: Abnormal pregnancy length (too short or too long) might be linked to complications and stress, possibly affecting postpartum depression risk.

