- Relax/sleep when newborn is tended: Ability to relax or sleep when newborn is tended suggests better coping, lower risk.  
- Marital status: Being married may provide emotional support; divorced status may increase risk. (Though only Married and Divorced present)  
- Feeling for regular activities: Feeling worried, tired, or afraid indicates emotional distress and higher risk; no negative feelings suggest lower risk.  
- Total children: More children might increase burden and stress, possibly increasing risk; however, first-time mothers may also have anxiety.  
- Monthly income before latest pregnancy: Higher income may reduce financial stress and risk; no or low income may increase risk.  
- Occupation After Your Latest Childbirth: Occupation may reflect social engagement and self-esteem; housewife status may correlate with higher risk due to isolation.  
- Fear of pregnancy: Fear related to pregnancy indicates emotional distress, increasing risk of postpartum depression.  

